1
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:10,890
JAMIE: Oracle down eight point seven six percent — and that's not even the headline.

2
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:29,879
ALEX: So the S&P ended down zero point five one percent, the Dow actually squeaked out a gain of zero point four one percent, but the Nasdaq got hit with one point zero seven percent down, and the Russell 2000 just sort of existed at minus zero point two one percent. The VIX spiked eight point nine one percent, so volatility came back like someone's ex at a grocery store you didn't expect.

3
00:00:29,879 --> 00:00:37,423
JAMIE: The Dow is up while everything else is struggling — that's the move of someone who just bought a house in a neighborhood they're pretty sure is fine.

4
00:00:37,423 --> 00:00:49,389
MAYA: Home Depot up three point six three percent, Walmart up three point three eight percent — so everyone's buying tools and groceries while the tech sector is getting liquidated. That's the portfolio of a person who's decided to become a prepper but only halfway committed.

5
00:00:49,389 --> 00:00:59,013
JAMIE: So the Supreme Court just rejected the DOJ's emergency request to block California from counting those disputed ballots. This is the federal government trying to intervene in a state's election process, and they got shut down.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/02/supreme-court-california-mail-ballots-election.html

6
00:00:59,013 --> 00:01:10,459
MAYA: Wait, so the federal government is basically showing up to state election offices like a parent trying to override a referee's call at their kid's soccer game, and the Supreme Court is just handing them a whistle and telling them to sit down.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/02/supreme-court-california-mail-ballots-election.html

7
00:01:10,459 --> 00:01:19,823
JAMIE: That's exactly it — and this sets a pretty clear precedent that even in urgent circumstances, the Court isn't going to let DOJ overrule how states process their own ballots. That's the real story here.

8
00:01:19,823 --> 00:01:29,448
ALEX: Apple's iPhone business just set a record — we're talking a quarter where they sold more iPhones than they ever have — but the stock is down anyway because Wall Street is worried about growth slowing.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/heres-why-palantirs-stock-is-down-despite-a-bombastic-quarter-42c9fdb8?mod=mw_rss_topstories

9
00:01:29,448 --> 00:01:40,373
JAMIE: So the thing that's supposed to make money is making more money than it ever has, and the market response is to sell it. That's the move of someone who ordered the expensive steak and then complained it was too filling.

10
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:49,738
MAYA: But here's the thing — Apple didn't even release a standard iPhone model this cycle. They just put out the Pro and Pro Max, and people still bought more of them than any iPhone ever.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/heres-why-palantirs-stock-is-down-despite-a-bombastic-quarter-42c9fdb8?mod=mw_rss_topstories

11
00:01:49,738 --> 00:01:57,802
ALEX: Which means the entire growth story is being driven by people trading up to more expensive hardware. That's sustainable until it isn't, and Wall Street's already pricing in the 'isn't' part.

12
00:01:57,802 --> 00:02:06,646
MAYA: So Apple's also hinting at new revenue streams through third-party AI app opportunities — basically admitting that the iPhone itself is maxed out and they need to start monetizing the ecosystem around it.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/heres-why-palantirs-stock-is-down-despite-a-bombastic-quarter-42c9fdb8?mod=mw_rss_topstories

13
00:02:06,646 --> 00:02:11,848
JAMIE: Translation: we've squeezed the hardware, now we're going to charge you rent for the software that runs on it.

14
00:02:11,848 --> 00:02:20,172
ALEX: A plane that crashed in Florida may have broken apart in mid-air before impact, and weather could have been a contributing factor — but investigators are still early in the investigation.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm369yy7lw73o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

15
00:02:20,172 --> 00:02:27,976
JAMIE: So the plane either fell apart because of the weather, or it fell apart and then the weather was there, and we won't know which one for a while.

16
00:02:27,976 --> 00:02:32,919
MAYA: So the Supreme Court basically told the DOJ: you can't call the ref, the ref is calling you.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/02/supreme-court-california-mail-ballots-election.html

17
00:02:32,919 --> 00:02:37,601
ALEX: And next time they try this, they'll know the answer before they even pick up the phone.

18
00:02:37,601 --> 00:02:41,243
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
